== Driver Skills Strategy
=== Scoring Priority

After reading and analyzing the initial version of the manual, I have developed some initial principles that I believe will be relevant despite any minor changes to the game rules in future updated game manuals. 

I will categorize my strategies in to 3 groups: _*Essentials*_, _*Priorities*_, and _*Additions*_.

_*Essentials*_ *must* be done within the allotted time. This can be done either at the beginning of the Skills attempt or at the very end, but these tasks need to be done regardless of order.

_*Priorities*_ will be the main focus for any remaining time that I have, excluding _*Essentials*_. This is where the majority of the active scoring will take place.

_*Additions/Extras*_ will only be done if all tasks in _*Priorities*_ are completed, and I can ensure I will have additional time even after accounting for the tasks in _*Essentials*_. 
#linebreak()
=== _Essentials_

==== Toggles

Scored points in non-center goals will only count if they are placed in the a goal in which the toggle's color corresponds to the quadrant of that goal. The reason that this is a necessity is simple: we cannot score only on the center goal, and if we want to score on any of the 8 other goals, we need to flip the toggles. 
#linebreak()
In a perfect Skills run, this would mean switching the two toggles in the blue quadrants to blue and the same for the two red toggles. However, since I am unsure whether we will use all 8 goals in our Skills run, this will be adjusted based on our needs. But the baseline still remains: we must change at least some number of toggles to effectively score points. I anticipate that this will be done either at the very beginning or end of the run, but the exact routing will be developed later on. 

==== Robot in the midfield or "Parking"

The rule states that any robot within the plane of the midfield in any way at the end of the Skills run will count for 8 points. This is simply just 8 free points; there is no reason not to go for this since it is very simple and easy to execute.
#linebreak()

=== _Priorities_
==== Scoring with only the yellow sides of pins

As opposed to scoring using the red/blue side of the pins, yellow pins are worth twice as more (10pts) compared to red/blue pins (5pts) when the toggle is activated in the corresponding quadrant or when the pin is scored in the middle of the field.
#linebreak()
#linebreak()
For *yellow/yellow* pins, either side can be used for scoring.
#linebreak()
#linebreak()
For *blue/yellow* or *red/yellow* pins, we will prioritize using the yellow side of the pin in scoring, due to the higher value of yellow pins.
#linebreak()
#linebreak()
For *blue/red* pins, these pins will only be used after we have used up all pins of the other types.
#linebreak()

=== _Additions/Extras_
==== Middle goal scoring
Though the middle goal has the benefit of not requiring a toggle to count scoring, since all previous rules apply, the vertical extension limit of the center plane is limited to only 18 inches, as opposed to 50 inches anywhere else on the field. Consequently, we cannot stack nearly as many pins and cups compared to the other 8 goals, so this will be a low-priority task.
==== Scoring with Blue/Red Pins
As previously mentioned, these pins will only be used when all other types of pins have been exhausted, so it is at the lowest of the priority list.
